PROGRAM 3 – Sliding Window Protocol and Go-Back-N
Aim: Implement flow control using the Sliding Window Protocol and loss recovery using Go-Back-N.

1. What is flow control?
Flow control regulates the speed of data transmission so that the sender does not overwhelm the receiver.

2. What is error control?
Error control detects and handles lost, damaged or duplicated frames during transmission.

3. What is the Sliding Window Protocol?
It allows multiple frames to be transmitted before receiving acknowledgements, improving transmission efficiency.

4. What is a sliding window?
It is a range of sequence numbers representing the frames that can be sent or received.

5. What is window size?
Window size is the maximum number of frames that can be transmitted without waiting for further acknowledgements.

6. What is Go-Back-N ARQ?
It is an error-control protocol in which the sender retransmits the lost or damaged frame and all subsequent outstanding frames.

7. What is ARQ?
ARQ stands for Automatic Repeat reQuest, a technique that uses acknowledgements and retransmissions for reliable communication.

8. What is acknowledgement (ACK)?
ACK is a message sent by the receiver to inform the sender that frames have been received successfully.

9. What is a sequence number?
It is a number assigned to each frame to identify it and maintain the correct order of transmission.

10. What is timeout?
Timeout is the maximum waiting period for an acknowledgement before the sender retransmits a frame.

11. What happens when a frame is lost in Go-Back-N?
The sender goes back to the missing frame and retransmits that frame and all subsequent outstanding frames.

12. What is Stop-and-Wait ARQ?
It sends one frame at a time and waits for its acknowledgement before sending the next frame.

13. What is Selective Repeat ARQ?
It retransmits only the specific lost or damaged frames instead of retransmitting all subsequent frames.

14. What is pipelining?
Pipelining allows multiple frames to be transmitted before acknowledgements arrive, improving channel utilization.

15. What is the difference between Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat?
Go-Back-N retransmits multiple outstanding frames, whereas Selective Repeat retransmits only the particular erroneous or lost frame.

16. What is the maximum sender window size in Go-Back-N?
For an \(m\)-bit sequence number, the maximum window size is \(2^m-1\).
