PROGRAM 9 – Frame Sorting Technique Used in Buffers
Aim: Write a program for frame sorting techniques used in buffers.

1. What is frame sorting?
Frame sorting is the process of arranging received frames in a proper order before processing them.

2. Why is frame sorting required?
It helps arrange frames correctly and ensures that data is processed in the proper sequence.

3. What is a buffer?
A buffer is a temporary memory area used to store data during transmission or processing.

4. What are the types of frames used in this program?
Numeric frames and text frames.

5. What is numeric frame sorting?
It arranges frames containing numbers in ascending or descending order.

6. What is text frame sorting?
It arranges text frames alphabetically.

7. Which sorting method is used for numeric data?
Numeric sorting compares numerical values and arranges them in the required order.

8. What is alphabetical sorting?
It arranges text in dictionary order, usually from A to Z.

9. What happens if frames arrive out of order?
They are rearranged into the correct order before further processing.

PROGRAM 10 – Wireshark Tool
Aim: Work with Wireshark for packet capture, starting Wireshark, viewing captured traffic, analysis, statistics and filters.

1. What is Wireshark?
Wireshark is a free, open-source network protocol analyzer used to capture and analyze network packets.

2. What is packet capturing?
Packet capturing is the process of collecting network packets transmitted and received through a network interface.

3. Why is Wireshark used?
It is used for network troubleshooting, protocol analysis, network security analysis and learning networking concepts.

4. Is Wireshark free or paid?
Wireshark is free and open-source software.

5. What is a network interface?
A network interface is a hardware or software connection through which a device communicates with a network.

6. What is a packet?
A packet is a formatted unit of data transmitted over a network.

7. What are the three main panes in Wireshark?
Packet List Pane, Packet Details Pane and Packet Bytes Pane.

8. What is the Packet List Pane?
It displays captured packets with details such as number, time, source, destination and protocol.

9. What is the Packet Details Pane?
It displays detailed protocol information of the selected packet.

10. What is the Packet Bytes Pane?
It displays the actual packet data in hexadecimal and ASCII formats.

11. What is a capture filter?
A capture filter specifies which packets should be collected during packet capturing.

12. What is a display filter?
A display filter shows only selected packets from the already captured traffic.

13. What is the filter used to display TCP packets?
tcp is used to display only TCP packets.

14. What is the filter used to display UDP packets?
udp is used to display only UDP packets.

15. What is the filter used to display ICMP packets?
icmp is used to display ICMP packets.

16. What is the filter used to display HTTP packets?
http is used to display HTTP packets.

17. What is a PCAP file?
A PCAP file is a file format used to save captured network packets for later analysis.

18. What is Protocol Hierarchy in Wireshark?
It displays the percentage and distribution of different protocols in captured traffic.

19. What is Conversations in Wireshark?
It displays communication statistics between pairs of network endpoints.

20. What is IO Graphs?
IO Graphs visually display network traffic and packet activity over time.

21. Which library is used by Wireshark for packet capturing on Linux?
Wireshark uses the libpcap library for packet capturing on Linux.

22. What is TShark?
TShark is the command-line version of Wireshark used to capture and analyze network packets.

PROGRAM 11 – Nmap Scanning and Operating System Detection
Aim: Show how to run Nmap scans and perform operating system detection using Nmap.

1. What is Nmap?
Nmap stands for Network Mapper. It is an open-source tool used to scan networks and discover active hosts and open ports.

2. Why is Nmap used?
It is used for network discovery, port scanning, service detection, OS detection and security auditing.

3. What is port scanning?
Port scanning is the process of checking network ports to identify which ports are open, closed or filtered.

4. What is host discovery?
Host discovery is the process of identifying active devices in a network.

5. What is an open port?
An open port is a network port on which an application or service is accepting connections.

6. What is a closed port?
A closed port is a port that is reachable but has no application accepting connections.

7. What is a filtered port?
A filtered port is a port whose status cannot be determined because a firewall or network filter blocks the probe.

8. What is service detection?
Service detection identifies the applications and their versions running on open ports.

9. What is OS detection?
OS detection identifies the operating system of a target device by analyzing its network responses.

10. What is the full form of Nmap?
Network Mapper.

11. Which command is used to scan a single host?
nmap 192.168.1.1 scans a single host.

12. Which command is used to detect service versions?
nmap -sV 192.168.1.1 detects service versions.

13. Which command is used for OS detection?
nmap -O 192.168.1.1 attempts to identify the target operating system.

14. Which command is used to scan a specific port?
nmap -p 80 192.168.1.1 scans port 80 of the specified host.

15. Which command is used to scan a range of ports?
nmap -p 1-1000 192.168.1.1 scans ports 1 to 1000.

16. What is SYN scanning?
SYN scanning is a TCP scanning technique that uses SYN packets to check port states.

17. What is the advantage of Nmap?
It is free, cross-platform, customizable and useful for network inventory and security auditing.

18. Which operating systems support Nmap?
Nmap supports Windows, Linux and macOS.

19. What information does Nmap output provide?
It provides host status, open ports, services, service versions and sometimes operating system information.

Note: Run scans only on systems or networks for which you have permission.

PROGRAM 12(i) – NS2 Simulator Introduction
Aim: Introduction to NS2 Simulator.

1. What is NS2?
NS2 stands for Network Simulator version 2. It is an open-source, event-driven tool used to simulate computer networks.

2. Why is NS2 used?
It is used to study network performance, packet transmission, packet drops, delay, throughput and congestion.

3. Is NS2 a hardware-based simulator?
No, it is a software-based simulator that allows us to study network behaviour without actual network hardware.

4. Which language is used to write NS2 simulation scripts?
TCL (Tool Command Language) is used to write NS2 simulation scripts.

5. What is NAM in NS2?
NAM stands for Network Animator; it visually displays network topology and packet movement.

6. What is a TCL file?
A TCL file is a script file containing instructions to create and run a network simulation in NS2.

7. What is a trace file?
A trace file (.tr) stores simulation events such as packet transmission, reception and dropping.

8. What is a NAM file?
A NAM file (.nam) stores simulation information used for graphical network animation.

9. Which protocols are supported by NS2?
NS2 supports protocols such as TCP, UDP, FTP, HTTP, AODV and DSR.

10. What are the main features of NS2?
It supports wired and wireless networks, multiple protocols, trace-file generation and graphical visualization using NAM.

11. What is a node in NS2?
A node represents a computer or network device in the simulated network.

12. What is a link in NS2?
A link represents the communication connection between two nodes.

13. What is a duplex link?
A duplex link allows data transmission in both directions between two nodes.

14. What is an agent in NS2?
An agent represents a transport-layer protocol such as TCP or UDP.

15. What is a traffic generator?
A traffic generator creates network traffic for transmission during simulation.

16. What is the command used to run an NS2 program?
ns filename.tcl is used to execute an NS2 simulation script.

17. What is the command used to open NAM?
nam filename.nam is used to view the network animation.

18. What are the advantages of NS2?
It is free, open-source and useful for testing network protocols and analyzing network performance.

19. What is packet drop?
Packet drop occurs when a packet fails to reach its destination, often because of congestion or buffer overflow.

20. What is throughput?
Throughput is the amount of successfully delivered data per unit of time.

21. What is network delay?
Network delay is the time taken by a packet to travel from source to destination.
